WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.323
Film Courage: Elaine, do you remember the
first time you heard about [the idea for]

00:00:02.323 --> 00:00:03.409
SPACE COMMAND [Movie]?

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.150
Elaine Zicree: Oh, my gosh!

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.730
Marc Zicree: [Laughs] Elaine Zicree: Well…no
I don’t.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.190
It seems to be ubiquitous.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.950
Marc Zicree: …It’s the hell you wondered
into [smiling].

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.120
Elaine Zicree: …He [Marc] has been talking
about it forever, you know?

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.449
But I actually don’t remember.

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:23.380
When did you first hit me up with that [laughing]?

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.830
Marc Zicree: Hhmmm??

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:32.050
Well you know it’s funny because with SPACE
COMMAND it evolved over a period of time.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.110
Basically as I said Star Trek was really a
formative moment in my life.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.350
When Star Trek debuted the first episode was
The Man Trap.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.739
It aired….I think Star Trek debuted when
I was 10 years old and it was the thing I

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:44.969
had been waiting all my life for it.

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:47.579
It really brought everything together.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:51.949
And I think from the moment that I read The
Making of Star Trek when I was 13, I decided

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:53.750
I wanted to be a show runner.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.640
I wanted to create and run my own science
fiction show.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.340
And all the hundred of hours of television
I did.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.989
All the books I wrote were all toward learning
that, gaining those skills that I would need

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:04.809
to be able to do that thing.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.500
And when Elaine and I got together…we’ve
been together for 40 years.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.780
We’ve been married for 39 [years].

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:11.860
She was a terrific director.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.710
She’d already been a director for an off-broadway
show.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:14.710
She’d already been an actress.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.850
She was a wonderful writer and so on some
projects we would collaborate and on other

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.110
projects we would work more individually over
the years.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:26.040
But clearly with SPACE COMMAND it obvious
that I would need her skill set.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.320
And we both love working with actors.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.360
We both love working with editors.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.160
I love visual effects artists.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.060
I love concept designers and so forth.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.520
So I had been gathering a team.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.049
So from the beginning I knew that I wanted
to work with Elaine on this project.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:44.040
Elaine Zicree: The key jumping off point was
we were both feeling disturbed about the dark

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.799
and cynical turn that science fiction had
taken and he [Marc] introduced me…I guess

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:54.049
I knew a little bit about it before, but he
was a great fan of things like Space Patrol

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:57.210
and it was where bad things happen but the
good people were good people.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.490
Marc Zicree: Well…just to jump in for a
second, Space Patrol was a show that aired

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.420
live in the 1950’s.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:09.020
And I didn't grow up watching it but in the
1980’s USA Cable [Network] had a show called

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.250
Night Flight and they ran kinescopes of that
show.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:17.380
Kinescopes were filmed…basically you’d
put a camera, a 16 mm camera on a high-resolution

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:23.710
TV screen as the show was broadcast live and
you would record it on film and that was called

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:24.710
kinescopes.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.680
And so they would air those kinescopes of
Space Patrol.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.300
It was a wonderful show.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.440
It came on before Star Trek.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.280
But it had a great sense of adventure and
later I met the start of the show Ed Kemmer

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.670
who had been a pilot in World War II.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.020
He flew a (North American Aviation) P-51 Mustang.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.520
He was captured by the Germans.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.220
Escaped from a German prisoner-of-war camp.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.873
He was a real hero before he played a science
fiction hero in the 1950’s.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.560
Elaine Zicree: And he looked like one.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.560
He seemed like one.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.560
Marc Zicree: A very handsome man.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.690
And so that was part of the inspiration and
then of course Star Trek.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.910
And what Elaine was talking about [regarding]
the dark science fiction, there was a period

00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:00.870
a few years ago where all the science fiction
was very dark and very dystopic, whether it

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:05.480
was Battlestar Galactica which I very much
liked or Elysium or After Earth or any of

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.480
these things.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.650
They all were saying “The future is going
to be really bad and there is not anything

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:09.670
you can do about it.”

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:13.780
Elaine Zicree: And that most people are mean-spirited
and things don’t work out.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.780
And what was odd and what Marc pointed out
that was odd, these were written by writers

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.050
whose lives were pretty darn good.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.140
Marc Zicree: Yeah, they would go home to their
families and they lived in Pacific Palisades.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.140
It was a very nice life.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.370
They were paid hundred of thousands of dollars
or millions of dollars.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.800
So some how there was a disconnect from the
life they were creating personally and the

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:31.800
work they were writing.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.751
And I felt very strongly that science fiction,
Outer Limits, Twilight Zone, Star Trek, those

00:03:36.751 --> 00:03:38.640
shows had a moral compass.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.640
And particularly Star Trek said “The future
is going to have challenges.”

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.770
Star Trek was shot during the civil rights
movement, during the Vietnam War and yet said

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:49.520
“The future is going to have challenges
but we can reach across boundaries and barriers

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.450
and come together to create something better.”

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.670
And I thought that was very important to say
now.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.450
Elaine Zicree: And some of it is not just
about science fiction, it’s the objective

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.450
of storytelling.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:02.550
I mean if we assume that most people have
kind of a hard life, that somebody in their

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.790
family is mentally ill, somebody is on substances.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:10.840
You struggle and you work hard and you watch
a story and it says “Well things are really

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.700
dark and awful and you don’t have any hope
and things are going to go to hell.”

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.940
And you say well is that really a responsible
stand to take or do you want your audience

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.560
to go out of there and say “Okay, it’s
a struggle but people can come out on top.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.950
People are basically good at heart if you
just give them a shot.”

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.060
And you give people a sense when they leave
that theater there is possibility.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.810
It just seems far more accurate and socially
responsible then to just put them on a downer.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:38.190
Marc Zicree: Well, you know, we’re working
in an extremely powerful medium.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.850
Television reaches millions of people around
the world.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.120
If you do it right, it lasts forever.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.230
Most of the shows I’ve written for are still
being watched even the ones I wrote when I

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.090
was in my early twenties.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:51.930
And so there is a question that many writers
never ask themselves, which is (as far as

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:56.600
I can tell)… which is “Am I actually creating
something which is making the world a better

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:57.600
place?

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.680
Am I actually sending out a message that I
would want people to take to heart?”

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.660
You know TV can do one of three things: It
can anesthetize or fill people with despair

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.190
or inspire people.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:15.170
And I think the first obligation of any dramatic
entertainment is to entertain, obviously.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.910
If you don’t entertain, it doesn’t matter
what your message is.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.350
People won’t stick around for it.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:23.050
But beyond that, once you entertain people,
once you bring them into your world and they

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:27.660
love that world and want to be part of that
world, then if you can say take these lessons,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.480
take what you’ve seen in this story, take
it out into the real world, take it out into

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.370
your life, you can actually have a better
life.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:33.630
You can actually make a better world.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.610
I think those are very important messages
to communicate.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.680
Elaine Zicree: Not airy-fairy.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.290
Not to sugar coat anything.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:42.510
Because people don’t like to be lied to.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:46.100
And they know when they’re being lied to
like “Oh, everybody is happy and everybody

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.730
is…” but within a real context where you
say “Okay, things are hard, you get into

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:55.920
a bad mood, you say the wrong thing but you
can be man enough to apologize.”

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.760
Those are the kind of things you’d like
to address.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:58.300
Question for the Viewers: Do you feel a responsibility
to inspire with your writing/storytelling?

